Now I have everything needed. Here's the formal audit using the **testsmells.org 19-smell academic catalog**, calibrated for integration tests.

---

## Test Smell Audit — `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs`

**Framework:** MSTest v4 · **.NET 10** · **Integration scope** (`[TestCategory("Integration")]`)

### Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High | 3 | 3 |
| Medium | 1 | 1 |
| Low | 0 | 0 |
| **Total** | **4** | **4 (of 7)** |

---

### Findings by Severity

#### ❶ HIGH — Assertion-Free Test (Unknown Test)

**Smell:** `Assertion Roulette → Assertion-Free Test`
**Location:** `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` (line 113–119)
**Risk:** Gives false coverage confidence — passes even if inserts silently fail.

```csharp
// BEFORE — no assertion, test always passes
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
}
```

**Fix:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_PersistsAllRecords()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));

    var users = _repository.ListAll();
    Assert.AreEqual(100, users.Count);
}
```

---

#### ❷ HIGH — Conditional Test Logic

**Smell:** `Conditional Test Logic`
**Location:** `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` (line 93–109)
**Risk:** The `if/else` branch means one assertion path is never exercised (a `User` is inserted, so the `PremiumUser` branch is dead code). This hides a testing gap.

```csharp
// BEFORE — branching means only one path is ever tested
if (loaded is PremiumUser premium)
{
    Assert.IsTrue(premium.DiscountRate > 0);
}
else
{
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
}
```

**Fix:** Write separate, focused tests for each type:
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_StandardUser_ReturnsExpectedName()
{
    _repository.Insert(new User("eve@example.com", "Eve"));
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");

    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded.Name);
}

[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_PremiumUser_HasPositiveDiscount()
{
    _repository.Insert(new PremiumUser("vip@example.com", "Vip", 0.15m));
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("vip@example.com") as PremiumUser;

    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.IsTrue(loaded.DiscountRate > 0);
}
```

---

#### ❸ HIGH — Sleepy Test

**Smell:** `Sleepy Test`
**Location:** `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` (line 82–90)
**Risk:** `Thread.Sleep(3000)` makes the suite 3 s slower and introduces non-determinism (timing-sensitive on CI). Even for integration tests, wall-clock sleeps are a smell when a polling/retry mechanism is feasible.

```csharp
// BEFORE
Thread.Sleep(3000);
Assert.IsTrue(_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id));
```

**Fix — poll with timeout:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public async Task NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay()
{
    var user = new User("dave@example.com", "Dave");
    _repository.Insert(user);

    var sent = await PollUntilAsync(
        () => _repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id),
        timeout: TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));

    Assert.IsTrue(sent, "Notification was not sent within timeout.");
}

private static async Task<bool> PollUntilAsync(Func<bool> condition, TimeSpan timeout)
{
    var deadline = DateTime.UtcNow + timeout;
    while (DateTime.UtcNow < deadline)
    {
        if (condition()) return true;
        await Task.Delay(100);
    }
    return false;
}
```

---

#### ❹ MEDIUM — Magic Number Test

**Smell:** `Magic Number Test`
**Location:** `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` (line 87) — `3000` ms
**Risk:** The literal `3000` has no documented relationship to the system's notification SLA. If the SLA changes, this number becomes a silent flakiness risk.

> **Note:** `Assert.AreEqual(3, users.Count)` in `ListUsers_ReturnsAllInserted` and `Assert.AreEqual(10, ...)` in `InsertUser_ConcurrentInserts` are **not** flagged — these are self-documenting counts directly following the number of inserts.

---

### Smells NOT Detected (Clean)

| Smell | Status |
|-------|--------|
| Mystery Guest | ✅ Clean — in-memory SQLite is hermetic, not an external resource |
| Eager Test | ✅ Clean — each test exercises ≤ 3 repository methods (appropriate for integration scope) |
| Sensitive Equality | ✅ Clean — no `.ToString()` comparisons |
| Exception Handling in Tests | ✅ Clean — no try/catch blocks |
| General Fixture | ✅ Clean — `_connection` and `_repository` are used by all 7 tests |
| Ignored/Disabled Test | ✅ Clean — no skipped tests |
| Duplicate Assert / Assertion Roulette | ✅ Clean — assertions are focused and meaningful |

---

### Positive Observations

- **Clear AAA structure** in `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase`, `UpdateUser_ModifiesExistingRecord`, `DeleteUser_RemovesFromDatabase`
- **Proper cleanup** — `[TestCleanup]` disposes the connection
- **Good naming** — method names follow `Action_Scenario` or `Action_Condition_Expected`
- **Hermetic in-memory database** — avoids external environment coupling
- **Concurrency test** (`InsertUser_ConcurrentInserts_MaintainsIntegrity`) is well-designed with proper `await`

---

### Prioritized Remediation Plan

| Priority | Smell | Fix Effort | Impact |
|----------|-------|-----------|--------|
| 1 | Assertion-Free Test | ~2 min | Eliminates false-confidence coverage |
| 2 | Conditional Test Logic | ~5 min | Removes dead assertion path |
| 3 | Sleepy Test | ~10 min | Saves 3 s/run, removes flakiness risk |
| 4 | Magic Number (3000) | Resolved by #3 | Poll timeout is self-documenting |

**Bottom line:** 4 of 7 tests are clean and well-designed. The 3 High findings are genuine false-confidence and reliability risks worth addressing. The remaining suite demonstrates solid integration test practices.
